  "body": "## Problem\nSome low-volume Vinted targets do not justify a dedicated account/bot. Existing bots support one product configuration, so one Vinted account cannot safely scan and negotiate several independent products with different filters, price ranges and negotiation ladders.\n\n## Changes\n- keep every existing bot's current configuration as the unchanged main product\n- add up to 4 optional additional products per bot (5 products total)\n- persist separate category/brand/model/query, price range, adaptive offer settings and negotiation steps per additional product\n- add frontend management for creating, editing, enabling/disabling and reviewing additional products\n- keep the Vinted account, password, browser session and daily negotiation budget shared by the bot\n- isolate discovered listing backlogs by product and persist the originating additional-target id on listings\n- use the originating product's negotiation ladder for backend negotiation decisions\n- scan active products sequentially in one authenticated Playwright session with rotating start order\n- keep a shared per-run real-action cap across all products\n- continue servicing conversations from disabled additional products with their original strategy\n- preserve the old single-product runtime path when a bot has no additional products\n\n## Isolation and safety\n- discovery, backlog reads and negotiation capacity are explicitly product-scoped\n- marketplace listing ownership cannot silently move between main/additional products\n- active negotiations of an additional product do not unnecessarily lock structural edits of the main product\n- shared Vinted account fields remain locked while any product has an active negotiation\n- main-product config changes only requeue main-product listings; account-identity changes still requeue account-specific CANNOT_NEGOTIATE rows across all products\n- shared daily budget is validated against every active product ladder\n- additional-product rows use cascading foreign keys so deleting a stopped bot cannot be blocked by target-owned negotiation steps/listings\n- schema initializer repairs pre-existing non-cascading additional-target foreign keys for local databases that already saw the feature branch\n\n## Compatibility\nExisting bots are not migrated into the additional-product table. Their current main product, credentials, sessions, listings and negotiation configuration remain in the existing schema. With zero additional products they continue through the original main-product endpoints and runtime behavior.\n\nInactive additional products are excluded from future catalog scans but stay in the Playwright payload so already-started conversations continue with the strategy that created them.\n\n## Tests\nRegression coverage includes:\n- zero-additional-product compatibility\n- active/inactive product execution plans\n- product-scoped discovery and backlog isolation\n- shared run action limits and daily-budget guards\n- additional-only negotiations vs main-product edit locks\n- main-only listing requeue behavior\n- account-wide CANNOT_NEGOTIATE refresh on identity change\n- cascading additional-target foreign keys\n- backend mapper/request validation and negotiation capacity behavior\n\nFinal CI run #622: Backend, Playwright unit tests, Frontend and Mobile all pass.",
